WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.585 --> 00:00:06.836
I had a friend,

00:00:06.878 --> 00:00:11.508
a songwriting mentor
and a great songwriter, John Stewart,

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:15.679
who told me just before he died,

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.765
"We are all just radios,

00:00:18.765 --> 00:00:21.935
hoping to pick up each other's signals."

00:00:23.103 --> 00:00:26.856
I found that to be true and profound.

00:00:27.274 --> 00:00:30.402
And I've spent my life
trying to clear the static.

00:00:32.237 --> 00:00:35.740
The strongest signals
that come to me are musical

00:00:35.782 --> 00:00:40.829
in the form of songs or stories
told in rhythm and rhyme.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:47.919
Sometimes I can tune in
to a revolution in the heart.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:55.135
Sometimes I hear a keen
from my Celtic ancestors.

00:00:55.969 --> 00:01:00.682
And some songs have been
postcards from my future.

00:01:04.436 --> 00:01:08.815
In my family, there was a song
for every loss, every celebration,

00:01:08.857 --> 00:01:11.735
every unspoken need, every longing.

00:01:13.028 --> 00:01:17.490
But there was also so much chaos
that often I was the only one

00:01:17.532 --> 00:01:20.619
who recognized
what was being communicated.

00:01:23.538 --> 00:01:27.876
My grandmother, Carrie Cash,

00:01:27.917 --> 00:01:31.546
moved with my grandfather
and their five children

00:01:31.588 --> 00:01:34.883
into a New Deal era colony

00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:38.595
created by FDR for poor families

00:01:38.595 --> 00:01:44.100
in the sunken lands
of the Arkansas Delta in 1935.

00:01:45.727 --> 00:01:49.773
So they moved into this freshly
painted cottage,

00:01:49.814 --> 00:01:53.610
and she gave birth
to two more babies there

00:01:53.652 --> 00:01:55.779
with the assistance only of a doctor

00:01:55.779 --> 00:01:58.323
who came by in a horse and buggy

00:01:58.365 --> 00:02:03.036
and pulled two loose aspirin
from his pocket to give to her.

00:02:03.995 --> 00:02:07.791
The same pocket in which he kept
his fishing worms.

00:02:07.791 --> 00:02:09.376
(Laughter)

00:02:11.795 --> 00:02:13.088
That's true.

00:02:13.088 --> 00:02:14.381
(Laughter)

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:20.553
I read once that every time
an old woman dies,

00:02:20.553 --> 00:02:22.889
a library disappears.

00:02:25.141 --> 00:02:27.268
And before her library disappeared,

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:30.438
I tuned into my grandmother's signals

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:36.736
and gleaned her tenacity

00:02:36.778 --> 00:02:38.405
which I borrowed,

00:02:40.156 --> 00:02:45.662
and her long suffering in her life
of constant work with seven children,

00:02:45.704 --> 00:02:48.206
six of whom made it to adulthood.

00:02:49.374 --> 00:02:52.752
In a house without electricity.

00:02:54.254 --> 00:02:57.132
In the sweltering cotton fields,

00:02:57.173 --> 00:02:59.259
and I wrote these words about her.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.678
(Music)

00:03:01.678 --> 00:03:04.889
(Singing) Five cans of paint

00:03:04.889 --> 00:03:07.475
in the empty fields

00:03:08.643 --> 00:03:11.187
And the dust reveals

00:03:16.443 --> 00:03:19.988
And the children cry

00:03:19.988 --> 00:03:22.782
the work never ends

00:03:23.908 --> 00:03:26.494
There’s not a single friend

00:03:32.208 --> 00:03:36.004
Who will hold her hand

00:03:36.004 --> 00:03:39.132
in the sunken lands?

00:03:56.524 --> 00:04:02.906
And the mud and tears
melt the cotton balls

00:04:04.491 --> 00:04:06.367
It’s a heavy toll

00:04:07.494 --> 00:04:08.787
Oh, oh

00:04:11.956 --> 00:04:15.460
His words are cruel

00:04:15.502 --> 00:04:18.087
and they sting like fire

00:04:19.464 --> 00:04:21.883
Like the devil’s choir

00:04:22.675 --> 00:04:24.302
Oh, oh

00:04:27.639 --> 00:04:31.351
Who will hold her hand

00:04:31.351 --> 00:04:34.687
in the sunken lands?

00:04:35.355 --> 00:04:40.151
(Music)

00:04:51.955 --> 00:04:57.752
The river rises and she sails away

00:04:59.462 --> 00:05:01.548
She could never stay

00:05:02.757 --> 00:05:04.133
Oh, oh

00:05:07.011 --> 00:05:10.974
Now her work is done

00:05:11.015 --> 00:05:14.602
in the sunken lands

00:05:15.937 --> 00:05:19.482
There’s five empty cans

00:05:19.858 --> 00:05:24.571
(Music)

00:05:36.082 --> 00:05:38.459
(Music ends)

00:05:40.753 --> 00:05:46.301
(Applause)

00:05:51.848 --> 00:05:54.267
In 1992,

00:05:54.309 --> 00:05:57.770
I was writing a song about my divorce,

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:59.522
which hadn't happened yet.

00:05:59.564 --> 00:06:01.441
(Laughter)

00:06:01.441 --> 00:06:05.528
But which I saw coming
like a freight train.

00:06:05.528 --> 00:06:08.114
(Laughter)

00:06:09.616 --> 00:06:12.452
So I was stuck in this song

00:06:12.452 --> 00:06:15.580
like I was stuck in my life.

00:06:15.622 --> 00:06:19.167
And the same time across the country,

00:06:19.167 --> 00:06:23.046
my mother was going through a box
of my school assignments

00:06:23.087 --> 00:06:26.507
and childhood drawings,

00:06:26.549 --> 00:06:28.259
and she sent it to me.

00:06:28.301 --> 00:06:32.388
So I started going through this box,
and I came across this yellowed paper,

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:37.101
this school assignment I had done
in the seventh grade

00:06:37.143 --> 00:06:39.103
in Catholic school

00:06:39.103 --> 00:06:42.190
on metaphors and similes.

00:06:43.316 --> 00:06:48.613
Now I vividly remember the pleasure
I took in that assignment

00:06:48.613 --> 00:06:50.698
because it was literally the only thing

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:53.660
the nuns had ever given me
to do that I enjoyed.

00:06:53.701 --> 00:06:56.120
(Laughter)

00:06:58.873 --> 00:07:01.834
So this line I had written
popped out at me.

00:07:03.211 --> 00:07:06.547
"A lonely road is a bodyguard."

00:07:09.384 --> 00:07:10.760
What did it mean?

00:07:12.178 --> 00:07:15.348
I had even pasted a picture
of this empty road

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:17.392
next to the line.

00:07:17.892 --> 00:07:19.185
It was a metaphor.

00:07:20.979 --> 00:07:26.818
It wasn't "a lonely road
is like a bodyguard," a simile.

00:07:27.652 --> 00:07:32.615
It was the much more poetic:
“A lonely road is a bodyguard.”

00:07:35.368 --> 00:07:41.374
As painful as that was then,
and as it still can be painful now,

00:07:41.374 --> 00:07:43.334
I knew what she was telling me.

00:07:43.376 --> 00:07:44.502
That ...

00:07:45.628 --> 00:07:50.133
Solitude can protect
the seeds of creativity.

00:07:50.925 --> 00:07:56.431
And that loneliness contains
a priceless gift

00:07:56.472 --> 00:08:00.977
if we can tolerate the initial discomfort

00:08:01.019 --> 00:08:04.522
and avoid the seduction of despair.

00:08:06.024 --> 00:08:10.028
So my 12-year-old waved at me
across the decades,

00:08:10.069 --> 00:08:13.406
saying that who I was

00:08:13.448 --> 00:08:15.575
was who I would become.

00:08:16.409 --> 00:08:18.077
And I waved back

00:08:18.119 --> 00:08:23.291
and I dropped her line
right in the song I was writing.

00:08:23.291 --> 00:08:29.255
(Singing) I’ll send the angels
to watch over you tonight

00:08:29.756 --> 00:08:33.468
And you send them right back to me

00:08:35.094 --> 00:08:38.806
A lonely road is a bodyguard

00:08:40.475 --> 00:08:42.518
If we really want it to be

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:43.811
(Music ends)

00:08:45.313 --> 00:08:48.941
We're all just radios

00:08:48.983 --> 00:08:52.028
hoping to pick up each other's signals.

00:08:52.445 --> 00:08:56.574
And some of those signals
have a backbeat and a melody,

00:08:56.574 --> 00:08:58.284
and they're universal.

00:08:58.951 --> 00:09:02.830
And music can unlock a frozen memory

00:09:02.872 --> 00:09:06.209
that melts into the seeds
of our creativity.

00:09:07.251 --> 00:09:09.545
And the reverse is also true.

00:09:09.962 --> 00:09:15.593
A memory can unlock a song
that's waiting to be written.

00:09:16.803 --> 00:09:22.767
It can reveal us to ourselves
across time and generations,

00:09:22.809 --> 00:09:25.937
in community, in solitude.

00:09:28.397 --> 00:09:31.567
Some frequencies are sound,

00:09:31.609 --> 00:09:34.320
and some frequencies are light.

00:09:35.029 --> 00:09:40.159
And some take a half a lifetime

00:09:40.201 --> 00:09:42.411
to reach their destination.

00:09:44.122 --> 00:09:48.042
(Music)

00:09:50.461 --> 00:09:55.216
(Singing) Light is particle and wave

00:09:58.136 --> 00:10:03.266
Our histories written large upon the page

00:10:04.433 --> 00:10:07.645
The star in middle age

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:11.983
The love that fades to black

00:10:12.024 --> 00:10:15.695
Once revealed, won’t be taken back

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:20.199
Light nothing can escape

00:10:23.786 --> 00:10:27.582
The ignorance we once forgave

00:10:29.959 --> 00:10:34.964
In the future,
if we don't decide to change

00:10:35.006 --> 00:10:37.800
The things we cannot save

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:42.096
But it slows to shine upon your face

00:10:42.722 --> 00:10:45.766
We owe everything

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:48.853
Everything

00:10:50.813 --> 00:10:52.398
Everything

00:10:52.773 --> 00:10:55.693
To this rainbow of suffering

00:10:57.236 --> 00:11:02.033
Light is particle and wave

00:11:04.827 --> 00:11:08.414
Refractions of this place

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:14.837
Reflections of our grace

00:11:15.671 --> 00:11:19.050
It reveals what we hold dear

00:11:19.091 --> 00:11:22.720
And it’s slow so I can hold you near

00:11:26.891 --> 00:11:28.017
(Music ends)

00:11:32.438 --> 00:11:38.236
(Applause)

00:11:38.653 --> 00:11:41.447
There's one thing I know for certain.

00:11:41.822 --> 00:11:46.244
That we clear the static
by being vulnerable

00:11:46.285 --> 00:11:48.037
and by telling the truth.

00:11:48.621 --> 00:11:51.791
And here we are doing just that.

00:11:52.667 --> 00:11:53.876
Thank you.

00:11:53.876 --> 00:12:00.883
(Applause)

